part.text:
I've created a server component that uses Next.js's `after` API (available in Next.js 15+ and stabilized in newer versions) to schedule non-blocking work after the response is sent.

I created the component at `app/Welcome.tsx` and integrated it into your main page.

### 1. Created `app/Welcome.tsx`
This component displays a welcome message and uses `after()` to log the page view asynchronously without delaying the initial response.

```tsx
import { after } from 'next/server';

async function logPageView() {
  // Simulate logging to an external analytics service
  console.log('Logging page view...');
  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 2000));
  console.log('Page view logged successfully!');
}

export default function Welcome() {
  // Schedule the logging task to run after the response is sent
  after(async () => {
    await logPageView();
  });

  return (
    <div className="p-8 font-sans">
      <h1 className="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">Welcome to our site!</h1>
      <p className="text-gray-600">
        We're glad you're here. Your visit is being anonymously logged for analytics.
      </p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 2. Updated `app/page.tsx`
I updated the main page to use the new `Welcome` component.

```tsx
import Welcome from './Welcome';

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main className="flex min-h-screen flex-col items-center justify-between p-24">
      <Welcome />
    </main>
  );
}
```

### Verification
I ran `npm run build` to confirm that the `after` API is available and functions correctly in your Next.js environment. The build passed, and the simulated logging output appeared in the console after the page generation.
